Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Infra Analysis", "GuideTitle",
 CellID->942062912,ExpressionUUID->"a3a3c038-ca24-47b7-9945-83f1c1312fe3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Infra analysis studies functions and flows on a substrate. A function on \
the vertices has a differential on the edges. A function is integrated over a \
region by summing it, one unit per vertex, and along a walk by summing it \
over the walk. Most objects are families of walks, so an integral along an \
object is itself a multi-object, one value per member. It is read as its \
value density, without enumerating the members. The integral of a \
differential ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{"d", "f"}], "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "4adb186d-3fe6-4a26-9a24-dcef501de2a8"],
 " has the single value ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["f", "TI"], 
   StyleBox["(",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   StyleBox["q", "TI"], 
   StyleBox[")",
    SpanMaxSize->1], "-", 
   StyleBox["f", "TI"], 
   StyleBox["(",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   StyleBox["p", "TI"], 
   StyleBox[")",
    SpanMaxSize->1]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "ac0dd6db-4615-4768-b64e-de375c2905d2"],
 " on every member from ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["p", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "5449e7be-677d-492b-ae3d-6a66149f6111"],
 " to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["q", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "8e5ef919-7710-4b62-a524-c542261ace3c"],
 "."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell["\<\
On a directed acyclic graph, or on any substrate seen from an origin, an \
antiderivative accumulates a function over the past of each vertex through a \
kernel of the incidence algebra. The derivative of the same method is its \
exact inverse; it is local exactly when the kernel sums over chains weighted \
on the links. The volume integral over the whole past is the exception: its \
inverse, the M\[ODoubleDot]bius derivative, reaches past the links wherever \
two chains with the same ends diverge. On the light-cone lattice that \
derivative is the discrete wave operator.\
\>", "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Infra analysis also studies the first-order structure of a substrate: the \
tangent space at a vertex, the displacements that move its vertices, and the \
tautological 1-form. A displacement of scale ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["r", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "dd8230ac-4fee-4ab7-879f-4460d5241a43"],
 " sends each vertex to a set of vertices within distance ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["r", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "7b6a0fcf-2dd5-4582-8aba-57c43ccdb52b"],
 ", a discrete flow for time ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["r", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "80f0a082-63d2-4708-8cd7-f4bf6eaa5e35"],
 ", and it is multivalued by design. Its operations are metric: scaling moves \
along the shortest paths from each vertex to its images, the sum is the \
bisector of the two orders of composition, and the commutator is the loop of \
two displacements and their inverses, or of their metric negatives. The \
automorphisms of the substrate are its Killing displacements. The tangent \
space is the tangent bundle, a fibration whose sections are vector fields, \
and the tautological 1-form pairs a vector with a covector."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"]
}, Open  ]],

